From: rive
Subject: [PATCH 0018] return balanced far call stub for aarch64 AOT

AOT text is mapped far from the runtime, so every call from AOT code to a
runtime function (aot_invoke_native for imports, aot_call_indirect, the
memmove and memset behind memory.copy and memory.fill) goes through a stub
the loader writes. The stub called its target with blr and then went back to
the AOT caller with br x30. A processor pairs bl with ret to predict return
addresses, and br x30 is not a return, so each such call left one stale entry
on the return stack. The next ret out of the AOT function then mispredicted,
and so did every return above it, about 5 ns each on Apple silicon.

Jump to the target through x16 instead, the register the procedure call
standard sets aside for veneers, so the target's own ret returns to the AOT
caller. A host to script callback that makes one import call went from 20 ns
to 12 ns in a bare WAMR harness, with discarded speculative work falling from
0.80 to 0.05 of useful work. x86-64 already jumps. The stub keeps its size,
and lives in the runtime, so existing AOT artifacts need no rebuild.

diff --git a/core/iwasm/aot/arch/aot_reloc_aarch64.c b/core/iwasm/aot/arch/aot_reloc_aarch64.c
--- a/core/iwasm/aot/arch/aot_reloc_aarch64.c
+++ b/core/iwasm/aot/arch/aot_reloc_aarch64.c
@@ -91,13 +91,10 @@
     uint32 i, num = sizeof(target_sym_map) / sizeof(SymbolMap);
     for (i = 0; i < num; i++) {
         uint32 *p = (uint32 *)plt;
-        *p++ = 0xf81f0ffe; /* str  x30, [sp, #-16]! */
-        *p++ = 0x100000be; /* adr  x30, #20; symbol addr is PC + 5 instructions
-                              below */
-        *p++ = 0xf94003de; /* ldr  x30, [x30]   */
-        *p++ = 0xd63f03c0; /* blr  x30          */
-        *p++ = 0xf84107fe; /* ldr  x30, [sp], #16  */
-        *p++ = 0xd61f03c0; /* br   x30          */
+        /* rive: a jump, so the callee's ret pairs with the caller's bl and
+           the return stack stays balanced; the item size is unchanged */
+        *p++ = 0x58000050; /* ldr  x16, #8 */
+        *p++ = 0xd61f0200; /* br   x16 */
         /* symbol addr */
         *(uint64 *)p = (uint64)(uintptr_t)target_sym_map[i].symbol_addr;
         p += 2;
